WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Spare Room: run the recruiter's code in a room with nothing in it.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:12.000
Binance's $6.34B loss involved an engineer running a recruiter's coding assignment on his work computer.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.600
A recruiter's test repo, opened in VS Code in your account. VS Code asks whether you trust its authors.

4
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.050
You trust it. VS Code then asks once whether the folder's tasks may run automatically, and you allow it.

5
00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:39.150
The folder's task runs by itself. This one is harmless: it checks 13 fake secrets, and as your account it reaches all 13.

6
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:43.284
Now with Wi-Fi off. The ping cannot reach the internet.

7
00:00:43.284 --> 00:00:54.815
Spare Room's scan of a bundled fake laptop still completes. It works fully offline and never reads the secrets' contents.

8
00:00:54.815 --> 00:01:01.315
Creating the spare room took 29.6 s (recorded earlier; Windows does not allow its permission prompt to be screen-recorded).

9
00:01:01.315 --> 00:01:04.336
The spare room: a separate, standard Windows account with nothing in it.

10
00:01:04.336 --> 00:01:16.742
Can the coding test run here? Node.js, npm and Git, yes. Python and VS Code need installing for all users.

11
00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:28.132
From the spare room, the 13 fake secrets kept in a copy inside your account's folder: all 13 blocked.

12
00:01:28.132 --> 00:01:43.547
Checked from inside the spare room: 12 of 12 secret locations in your account are out of reach. One .env outside any account folder is still readable by everyone.

13
00:01:43.547 --> 00:01:51.047
13 reachable, then 0. Offline. Never reads secret contents. Weaker than a virtual machine. spare-room-demo.netlify.app
